  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 337 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAs we move towards the mid-late part of the week, guidance indicates \nthe anomalous mid-level ridge nudges northwest, encompassing more of \nthe region. There is good consensus that the boundary retreats \npoleward into NE and IA, placing our area further into the warm \nsector away from rain chances. Extremely warm 850mb temps around 20C \nmean that confidence is high in the continuation of near record heat \ninto the weekend with highs in the 90s across most of the area. \nRecord highs may be threatened at STL and COU Thursday and Friday \n(Please refer to the CLIMATE section below). Temperature IQR spreads \non the LREF remain around only 5 degrees through Friday, \nillustrating that extremely hot conditions will continue to impact \nthe area into the weekend.\n\nThere continues to be increasing signal among long-range guidance \nthat the strong mid-level ridge breaks down as a longwave trough \nswings across the Intermountain West on Saturday. It appears that \nSaturday will remain hot for a majority of the area with IQR \ntemperature spreads increasing to 15-20 degrees on Sunday, \nindicating the potential for a cold front bringing cooler air into \nthe region. By next week, IQR temperature spreads decrease again to \n5-10 degrees, but this time centered on values near climatological \nnormals. The 25th percentiles indicates the potential for highs in \nthe 70s with the 75th percentile representing values that are in the \nupper 70s/lower 80s. All of this reveals the signal is there for an \nend to the heat by the start of next week.",
